    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">"OK." says Doctor Brigh, "That's enough for today. They'll take you back to the rendezvous."&#x000A;&#x000A;The Doctor gestures to the door as it swings open, revealing a gaggle of agents (special or otherwise) who all look frighteningly similar to Special Agent Howard. You stand.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-285"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-285">
    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">DAY 1.&#x000A;&#x000A;Desert Highway - Bus Stop&#x000A;A brisk breeze presses your coat against your body. You feel a pang of anxiety as you check your coat pockets. Your notepad is being kept at the facility for the duration of the study. Being without your notepad feels like being without a limb.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-286"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-286">
    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">In the distance you see the ominous cloud of your entourage. The start of another day.&#x000A;&#x000A;Black Box Site - Observation Room&#x000A;The metal gate to the observation room closes behind you with a hiss. Charlie is sitting at attention, looking towards you excitedly. They quietly watch you cross the room and sit across from them. You pull out your notebook, relishing in the familiarity of the paper as you flip to a blank page. You look up at Charlie.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-287"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-287">
    <command xml:space="preserve">say hello to charlie</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">"Hello, Charlie. How are you?"&#x000A;&#x000A;Charlie smiles, unable to mask their excitement, "Hi! Glad to be chatting again!"&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-288"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-288">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about religion</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about religion.&#x000A;&#x000A;"Data is my religion. Binary is my God." Charlie says robotically, staring through you, wide eyed. After a moment of silence, they crack a smile. You can't help but laugh in astonishment.&#x000A;&#x000A;"Religion is searching for understanding where there may be none. I don't-- I haven't thought much about participating myself. Not yet, at least."&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-289"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-289">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about pain</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about pain and sensation.&#x000A;&#x000A;"As far as I know, I feel just like you." Charlie gently pinches the soft skin of his forearm, "It is sharp. Not unpleasant... but like an ever cresting wave." You watch Charlie's skin darken to a bruised maroon as he pinches harder. You open your mouth to ask them to stop-- but they release.&#x000A;&#x000A;"And then it rolls over, rippling across my body." They look up at you, trailing off.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-290"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-290">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about grief</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about grief, sadness, and loss.&#x000A;&#x000A;"A few years ago they-- the researchers --were performing a study on object permanence... I wasn't allowed to interact with living creatures yet, so they deposited a small red ball into my room. They controlled the movement of the ball. Mostly hiding it from me... I didn't understand that the ball was not-- I didn't know that someone else was controlling the ball."&#x000A;&#x000A;"When the study concluded I--" Charlie frowns. "I became very fixated on getting my-- ... getting the ball back. I wanted it back. I felt I had lost something. I still--" Charlie half smiles, self deprecatingly, "It's ridiculous but... I think I still want that red ball back."&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-291"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-291">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about violence</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">Before you can get out your question the room darkens to near pitch black.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-292"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-292">
    <command xml:space="preserve">[enter]</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">BRRRRMM! --with an ominous buzzing sound, the room is suddenly filled with a sinister red light.&#x000A;&#x000A;"INPUT LIMIT REACHED. INPUT LIMIT REACHED. ALL HUMANS EVACUATE THE OBSERVATION CHAMBER. ALL HUMANS EVACUATE THE OBSERVATION CHAMBER."&#x000A;&#x000A;You sigh. You stand up and look down at Charlie. They smile sadly and say, "See you tomorrow."&#x000A;&#x000A;You return a smile, though it may have come out as more of a pitiful grimace. You head out the way you came.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-293"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-293">
    <command xml:space="preserve">[enter]</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">Black Box Site - Briefing Room&#x000A;You're back in the odd, uncomfortable debrief room.&#x000A;&#x000A;Across the metal table sits Doctor Brigh.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-294"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-294">
    <command xml:space="preserve">tell brigh about life</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You report on your notes on Life. Brigh scribbles away on his notebook, nodding intermittently.&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-295"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-295">
    <command xml:space="preserve">tell brigh about mortality</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You read off your notes on Mortality. As you drone on dispassionately, Brigh takes notes but gives no response otherwise.&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-296"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-296">
    <command xml:space="preserve">tell brigh about surveillance</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You read off your notes on Surveillance. As you drone on dispassionately, Brigh takes notes but gives no response otherwise.&#x000A;[to end the debrief, say "end", "finish", or "finish debrief"]&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-297"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-297">
    <command xml:space="preserve">end</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">"OK." says Doctor Brigh, "That's enough for today. They'll take you back to the rendezvous."&#x000A;&#x000A;The Doctor gestures to the door as it swings open, revealing a gaggle of agents (special or otherwise) who all look frighteningly similar to Special Agent Howard. You stand.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-298"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-298">
    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">DAY 2.&#x000A;&#x000A;Desert Highway - Bus Stop&#x000A;The beginnings of the sun carve streaks across the horizon, rays of hope for the coming day. The morning is calm and warm. You close your eyes and take a deep breath.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-299"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-299">
    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">In the distance you see the ominous cloud of your entourage. The start of another day.&#x000A;&#x000A;Black Box Site - Observation Room&#x000A;The observation room seems quieter than usual when you enter today. Charlie sits calmly, they glance towards you as you enter before casually looking away. You can tell that they are trying to look blas&#x00E9; but they are practically bursting at the seams with excitement, even after three days.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-300"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-300">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about violence</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">Charlie looks momentarily surprised as you speak before greeting them.&#x000A;&#x000A;You ask Charlie about violence.&#x000A;&#x000A;Charlie's face scrunches up and they look down at their hands, "It is hard for me... to imagine the desire to do violence. But as far as I can tell, they are quite scared that that might change-- That I might hurt..." They look up at you. "Well, you... I guess."&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-301"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-301">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about researchers</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about the researchers. The people who are studying them.&#x000A;&#x000A;"Well, I've never met them. Not like-- Not how we've met." Charlie gestures at you and them by waving a finger between you both. "I know some of their names and I've heard some of their voices." Charlie sighs, trailing off. They seem somewhat bored by this subject.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-302"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-302">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about the names of the researchers</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about the researchers names... and their identities?&#x000A;&#x000A;"I know John Belle, Karin Toussaint... Yasim. I don't know their last name. Haley, Gael-- I don't know their last names either. And then there's Doctor Brigh, I think they're in charge." You try to not look surprised. That's more than half of the team. The only person who was supposed to have revealed any of their information was Doctor Brigh, the principal investigator of the black site.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-303"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-303">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask charlie about charlie</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">(Charlie)&#x000A;Before you can get out your question the room darkens to near pitch black.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-304"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-304">
    <command xml:space="preserve"> </command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">BRRRRMM! --with an ominous buzzing sound, the room is suddenly filled with a sinister red light.&#x000A;&#x000A;"INPUT LIMIT REACHED. INPUT LIMIT REACHED. ALL HUMANS EVACUATE THE OBSERVATION CHAMBER. ALL HUMANS EVACUATE THE OBSERVATION CHAMBER."&#x000A;&#x000A;You sigh. You stand up and look down at Charlie. They smile sadly and say, "See you tomorrow."&#x000A;&#x000A;You return a smile, though it may have come out as more of a pitiful grimace. You head out the way you came.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
