  <item nodeId="node-381">
    <command xml:space="preserve">[enter]</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">This is a game created for Narrative Studio as a part of the Game Design MFA program.&#x000A;&#x000A;LOGLINE&#x000A;When you, a researcher of artificial intelligence, are suddenly asked to participate in cutting-edge research for the government you immediately, albeit anxiously, jump at the opportunity. You are tasked with studying CHARLIE, an android who seemingly acquired consciousness and is learning at a highly accelerated rate.&#x000A;&#x000A;DETAILS AND INSPIRATION&#x000A;CHARLIE is inspired by Neofeud, a point-and-click adventure game set in a dark cyberpunk world. Neofeud is solo-developed by Christian Kealoha Miller, a kanaka maoli (native Hawaiian) artist and activist. In Neofeud androids are underprivilidged and oppressed they lack sentience, relegated to the gutter and deprived of basic rights. &#x000A;&#x000A;CHARLIE follows a similar vein: it is a game about seeing through the fear-mongering and dehumanization, about the fortress of bureaucracy that lies between opressed people and their freedom, and about the feeling of hopelessness in the face of institutionalized, systematic oppression.&#x000A;&#x000A;In terms of form and structure, CHARLIE is heavily inspired by Galatea, a beautiful game by Emily Short, who needs no introduction. While playing Galatea I found myself exclusively asking questions and so thought to partition the acts of "asking" and "telling".&#x000A;&#x000A;&#x000A;&#x000A;Please press SPACE to continue.</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-382"/>
    </children>
  </item>
