  <item nodeId="node-902">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask about brigh</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">(addressing Charlie)&#x000A;Charlie looks momentarily surprised as you speak before greeting them.&#x000A;&#x000A;You ask Charlie about Doctor Brigh.&#x000A;&#x000A;"I know that they are the principal investigator in this facility... I still remember the first thing they said to me--" Charlie puts on a faux frown and speaks in a gruff voice. "No Turings for you Charlie. I know you'd pass. No point in doin" that." The impression takes you so by surprise that you can't help but laugh. It's actually quite a good impression. You're sure that Charlie would be capable of perfectly recreating the Doctor's voice... but they elected instead to create a caricatured, flawed impersonation.&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-903"/>
      <child nodeId="node-919"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-903">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask about life</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about life and birth: newness and creation.&#x000A;&#x000A;"As in... Am I alive?" You pause, watching Charlie sort through their own rhetorical question. Just as you consider intervening--&#x000A;&#x000A;"I remember everything with perfect clarity. From the moment I was first activated to now. To me it is memory that makes up my life. Perhaps it is the same for you." Charlie glances conspicuously at your notepad, "Perhaps your memory also shapes your life."&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
    <children>
      <child nodeId="node-904"/>
    </children>
  </item>
  <item nodeId="node-904">
    <command xml:space="preserve">ask about mortality</command>
    <result xml:space="preserve">You ask Charlie about death and its inevitability: the concept of mortality.&#x000A;&#x000A;"I don't age... but I feel that some day I will die..." Charlie pauses. They look up at the ceiling, thinking for a moment. "But I like for things to end... Not in a mean way. It just wouldn't be so special unless at some point, quite tragically, it wasn't."&#x000A;&#x000A;&gt;</result>
    <commentary xml:space="preserve"></commentary>
    <changed>NO</changed>
